  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 218 PM MDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\nNot much has changed in this version of the long-term forecast other \nthan the addition of next Saturday into the mix. Seasonably pleasant \nweather is expected to continue through next week into the weekend \nas a longwave ridge influences our area. Upper-level flow is still \nexpected to be from the northwest for most of the week. Ensemble \nguidance continues to show a shift in flow briefly to the north \nWednesday, back to northwest Thursday/Friday, then zonal westerly as \nthe ridge axis passes over the Great Plains. A few shortwave \npassages are possible during the four-day forecast period. A frontal \nboundary should move through the region Tuesday into Wednesday, \nshifting the surface winds. Guidance doesn't project precipitation \nas QPF is 0 for pretty much the entire County Warning Area (CWA).\n\n\nProbability of Precipitation (PoPs) are likely to be less than 5% \nfor the entire Tri-State region through Saturday of next week. Dry \nair aloft plus high pressure is expected to keep any cloud \ndevelopment limited to diurnal cumulus. Cloud coverage guidance does \nnot anticipate more than 40% through Saturday so plentiful sunshine \nis expected. High temperatures are forecasted in the upper 70s to \nmid 80s with Tuesday being the warmest day across the CWA. Modest \ndew points in the 40s/50s for the majority of the CWA are expected \nwith some areas east of Kansas Highway 25 seeing up to low 60s.\n\n\nFire weather potential will start to become a concern next week as \nRelative Humidity (RH) percentages fall into the 20s throughout the \neastern Colorado counties. Tuesday will be a day to monitor as \nthere's a chance to see values dip into the teens in this area. \nSurface winds are not as impressive Tuesday as 10-15 mph with gusts \nup to 25 mph are likely. RH should continue to linger around the 20s \nwest of US-385 heading into the weekend. Simultaneously, winds 10-25 \nmph, gusting up to 35 mph in a few spots is expected for this area \nWednesday through Saturday. For the rest of the CWA, a general 5-15 \nmph wind is likely. Gusts up to 25 mph with RH in the 30-50 \npercentiles should be expected everywhere at or east of K-27.",
